H=u("p"),P=d("In practice, nobody enumerates orderings. Many orderings put the same people\n  ahead of "),D=new z(!1),F=d(" and differ only in how everyone is shuffled\n  around it, and those orderings all produce the same marginal contribution. If\n  we count the duplicates ("),j=new z(!1),_=d(" ways to arrange the people\n  before, and "),V=new z(!1),U=d(" ways to arrange the people\n  after), the sum collapses from "),W=new z(!1),G=d(" terms to\n  "),Y=new z(!1),X=d(":"),K=p(),Z=u("div"),J=p(),Q=u("p"),ee=d("That fraction isn't a mysterious weight. It's simply the fraction of orderings\n  in which exactly the people in "),te=new z(!1),re=d(" got there first. With\n  three players, it says that a marginal contribution to the empty set counts for\n  "),ne=new z(!1),ae=d(", a contribution to a single colleague counts\n  for "),oe=new z(!1),ie=d(", and a contribution to the pair counts for\n  "),se=new z(!1),le=d(". That's because arriving first and arriving\n  last each happen in a third of the orderings, while the middle third is split\n  between the two colleagues who could have arrived first."),he=p(),ce=u("h1"),ce.textContent="Why this average and not another?",ue=p(),me=u("p"),me.textContent="Averaging over orderings sounds reasonable, but so did the two answers it\n  replaced. The reason to prefer it is a theorem, and that theorem is about four\n  properties.",de=p(),pe=u("div"),fe=u("div"),ge=u("h4"),ge.textContent="Efficiency",ve=p(),ye=u("div"),be=p(),xe=u("p"),xe.textContent="The payouts add up to exactly what there is to share. Nothing is invented\n      and nothing is left unclaimed, which is where both of our first attempts\n      failed.",we=p(),ke=u("div"),Me=u("h4"),Me.textContent="Symmetry",Se=p(),ze=u("div"),Ae=p(),Te=u("p"),Te.textContent="If swapping two players never changes what a group is worth, they get paid\n      the same. Names carry no information; only the value function does.",qe=p(),Ce=u("div"),Ne=u("h4"),Ne.textContent="Null player",B